{"id": "sllama0164", "pool": "sllama", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3913370498, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "f40edaa94b82cc480a8162abcd570effa331c0a9b7ca31632511f1cd5b9e06ff", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 4, "query": "How many gurus are there in Sikhism?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/sllama0164.wav", "input_sha256": "598fb938790be51b72d4a004b712f3cb52fa3ab12a4eaa51f67881d22dfb2ed5", "input_samples": 40332, "input_s": 2.521, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.7225, "act": 0.9994, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "There are many g urus in Sikhism. The Sikh tradition recognizes a long line of spiritual teacher s known as Gurus, starting with Guru Nan ak Dev Ji, the found er of Sikhism. In total, there are ten recognized Gurus who led and guided the faith. These include Gu ru Angad Dev Ji, Guru Am ar Das Ji, Gu ru Ram Das Ji, Guru Arjan Dev J i, Guru H argobind Ji, Gu ru Har Rai J i, Guru Har kr om Ji, Gu ru T harun da r Ji, Gu ru Har Krish an Ji, and finally Gu ru Gobind Singh J i, who established the Khalsa in 1 6 99 . So, while there is only one living Guru in the traditional sense today—the Granth Sahib, the holy scripture—the de ad Gurus continue to be revered as central figures in Sikh history and spiritual leadership.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 52, "feed_lag_max_ms": 123, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 239, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/sllama/audio/sllama0164_balanced_local.wav", "sha256": "8db8676d3824f89b3afbef0af0395021d19dd8694096